WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.500
Speaker 1: The scary part is that Herschel
Walker had a chance and he almost won.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.550
I've been saying now for months that Herschel
Walker never should have even had a shot in

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:15.000
a country that made sense with voters with
some connection to reality.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.869
Someone like Herschel Herschel Walker never
should have even won the Republican nomination

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:26.500
in Georgia to be senator, never mind getting
49% of the vote roughly in the general election

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.840
in Georgia, which is what happened in the
runoff.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.580
And there's a couple of good articles and
one that I want to highlight in particular

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:32.970
that makes this case.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:37.809
Jack Holmes wrote a couple of days ago in
Esquire.

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:43.760
Herschel Walker lost, but it's a very bad
sign that he had any chance at all.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:47.039
There was a shark in the pool and nobody got
bit.

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:50.280
But why were so many people cheering for the
shark in the pool?

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:55.920
And the article makes points that are not
altogether surprising.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.380
But the point that we've been making and that
Jack Holmes makes is that although it's a

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:05.180
good thing that Herschel Walker lost, he barely
did.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:09.659
And the fact that he almost became one of
100 senators in the United States is certainly

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:14.840
emblematic and indicative of a much bigger
and scarier problem in the United States.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:19.049
And in the article, Jack Holmes writes, It
feels almost unnecessary at this point to

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:24.050
run through Herschel Walker's record as a
national political candidate.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:28.229
He's running in Georgia, but said in a campaign
speech he lives in Texas, etc., etc..

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:31.660
But as you read down the article.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:36.970
It explains the answer for a lot of people
to the question why make Herschel Walker a

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:42.980
senator seem to be that if Walker wasn't elected,
those people, Democrats, Marxists.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:47.310
You know, who the hell knows would control
the Senate?

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:53.240
And by the time that the election came up
on Tuesday, that was no longer the issue,

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:58.180
because we knew that at minimum, Democrats
would have a 5050 majority with Kamala Harris

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.510
as the tie breaking vote.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:08.880
And so it became more about who gets power
and who do Republicans vote for and what are

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.720
the principles here and who are the backers
of this guy?

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.810
And when you zoom out and you think about
what happened, you realize that this was really

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:21.940
the worst of the worst of Republican politics
at this point in time.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.160
What do I mean by that?

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.250
Well, it's basically at least three different
things.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:35.530
Number one, for many Republican voters and
for the GOP establishment, the RNC, at the

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:41.840
end of the day, it's simply about is there
a Democrat that we can prevent from winning

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:42.840
somewhere?

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.569
It doesn't matter who we put in place.

00:02:44.569 --> 00:02:48.599
So the No Democrats ever is a big part of
this.

00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:53.970
And later on in this in this part of the show,
we're actually going to look at a Republican

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:57.280
who is saying it's not about no Democrats
no matter what.

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.020
It actually does depend who the Republican
is.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:00.140
And Mitt Romney is that guy.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:01.159
We'll get to that a little bit later.

00:03:01.159 --> 00:03:02.159
So you've got the.

00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:03.400
But listen, war knocks a Democrat.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.400
We can't have that.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:05.400
That's number one.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.069
Number two, you have the MAGA non MAGA split.

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:09.830
So it has to be acknowledged that.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:16.070
The other factor here is that Herschel Walker
is sort of a MAGA cult type candidate.

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:21.849
And as we know from following Trump in 2016
and 2020 and into 2022 midterms, through his

00:03:21.849 --> 00:03:25.850
endorsements and now as a 2024 candidate,
it is a cult.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:30.650
And one of the things about the cult is when
the cult leader tells you this is the guy

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:35.650
in this particular case, you accept that,
you accept that, and you don't question it.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:42.189
And Donald Trump said this is the guy and
it failed, fortunately, but the MAGA cultists

00:03:42.189 --> 00:03:43.189
accepted it.

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:44.480
So that's another layer to this.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.290
You've got better than a Democrat no matter
what cult candidate.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:50.329
Okay.

00:03:50.329 --> 00:03:57.230
And then you have the third part of this,
which is the voters more broadly.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:03.959
And this goes beyond cult or not cult, but
it's the general problem in the United States

00:04:03.959 --> 00:04:09.189
of how there really is no bar.

00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:10.189
There's no.

00:04:10.189 --> 00:04:11.189
Speaker 2: Bar for.

00:04:11.189 --> 00:04:15.870
Speaker 1: The majority of the Republican
electorate.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:16.870
Big picture.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:23.000
Now, that doesn't mean they'll literally vote
for anyone in a primary.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.120
But Trump 2016 was instructive of that.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.780
Why did Trump run as a Republican and not
as a Democrat?

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:34.440
He really was, at least loosely speaking,
a Democrat.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:41.130
It seems most of his life, but it was because
there is some standard on the Democratic Party

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.130
side.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:47.570
It's not anyone can get the nomination, just
if they come up with the right things to say,

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:52.759
even if they completely contradict 68 years
of their lives, as did Trump on issues of

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:55.509
religion and abortion and other things.

00:04:55.509 --> 00:05:02.860
The Republican Party has no such bar, and
so because of that, we saw that they were

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:08.460
gullible enough to go for it in 2016 and then
it was sort of a path dependance that got

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.070
them there in 2020 as well.

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:12.060
Those are really the three things that this
is about.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.509
And as it applies to Herschel Walker, we're
all glad he lost.

00:05:15.509 --> 00:05:20.139
Herschel Walker in the U.S. Senate would be
a humiliation to this country and it would

00:05:20.139 --> 00:05:22.510
be a humiliation in so many different ways.

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:23.510
Great.

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:24.920
We dodged the bullet and not by very much.

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:28.870
I think it's important to say under 100,000
votes, if I recall incorrectly.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:31.229
But he had a shot.

00:05:31.229 --> 00:05:36.039
And as we saw in 2016 with Trump, sometimes
you don't dodged the bullet.

00:05:36.039 --> 00:05:37.670
And then in 20, we did dodge it.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:44.319
And so let's not take away there's people
emailing me saying we made it, we made it.

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:45.319
The 2022 midterms.

00:05:45.319 --> 00:05:46.400
They weren't as bad as they could have been.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:52.110
Yes, they weren't as bad as they could have
been, but they were almost so bad that they

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:54.100
could be that bad or worse in 2024.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:06:20.039
We don't want 

00:06:20.039 --> 00:07:13.370
to allow that 
to happen.

